Double-entry accounting has a reputation for being intimidating, but the core idea is simple: every transaction touches two accounts, not one.

## Every rupee, twice

Spend 4,500 on groceries from your card, and two things happened: your groceries expense went up by 4,500, and your card balance went up by 4,500 too (you owe more). One entry, two sides — a debit and a credit that always balance to zero.

## Why this matters more than it sounds

Because both sides must always balance, an error or an attempt to quietly alter a number shows up immediately as an imbalance. It's the same discipline that's kept real accounting honest for centuries — Salli just automates the bookkeeping so you never have to think about which side is which.

## What it means for you

You never have to understand debits and credits to use Salli day-to-day. Type "spent 4,500 on groceries" and the correct double-entry gets drafted for you. But the rigor underneath is exactly why the numbers you see are ones you can trust.
